  nonce: e048  # e048
  data:  # e048
    kind: work_item  # e048
    title: "smart-test --narrow keeps tests per changed FILE, not per changed block, and checks coverage whole-file; make it block-level with a block-scoped coverage verdict and a fresh index, then default it on locally (CI manifest unchanged)"  # e048
    status: todo_soft  # e048
    priority: 3  # e048
    parent:  # e048
    tags:  # e048
    - smart_test  # e048
    - ci_infrastructure  # e048
    isbefore: []  # e048
    duplicate_of: []  # e048
    not_duplicate_of: []  # e048
    pr_ref:  # e048
    description: "Goal: a one-line edit to one function runs, LOCALLY, only the tests observed executing that function, with no false coverage red, so `smart-test --narrow` can become the local default. The CI manifest is untouched: it is snapshotted from the import-graph slice before any coverage phase (`MANIFEST_TESTS=\"$AFFECTED_TESTS\"` in scripts/smart-test, pinned by test_smart_test_manifest_split.py, per the owner's 2026-08-16 option A on WI-kuliv / WI-bolot), so CI keeps running the un-narrowed set and stays the backstop.\n\nToday (WI-bolot, done): `--narrow` is opt-in and its keep set is FILE-level. `touching_test_files` keeps every test observed executing ANY block of a changed file, because the local coverage gate is whole-file (selection_index.py module docstring, \"RUN-SET HELPERS AND NARROWING\"). So a one-line edit to io_boundary.py keeps every test that touches any block of io_boundary.py, and a comment-only edit keeps the same set. block_hash.py already computes per-block digests that ignore comments and line shifts; the selection just does not use them at block granularity.\n\nThree changes, in order:\n1. Block-level keep set: keep the tests observed executing a CHANGED block (digest moved, or block vanished/renamed per `changed_blocks`), not any block of a changed file. New blocks with no history keep the file-level rule (no evidence yet).\n2. Scope the local coverage verdict to what narrowing claims: when narrowed, require 100% on the changed blocks' lines, not the whole changed file. Without this, (1) turns every narrowed run red on the unchanged blocks it no longer exercises. The un-narrowed whole-file gate stays as is (and CI's per-PR job is unaffected).\n3. Keep the index fresh, so the keep set is rarely short a test: e.g. fold coverage from the twice-daily full-suite run (or a scheduled local --full) into selection_index. The index only knows tests it has OBSERVED running a block, so between full runs it can miss one; CI's un-narrowed run catches any miss at the cost of a round trip.\nThen flip SELECT_NARROW's default to true locally, with a `--no-narrow` escape.\n\nPrerequisite (WI-zajuv, isbefore): the set of tests that read a source file as TEXT rather than executing it (field-axis gate, citation/line-number tests per WI-lujon, docstring checks). Under a block-level keep set a comment-only edit keeps nothing, and a text-reading test that executes no block of the file is dropped. That hazard partly exists in today's --narrow already (a text-reader that imports other modules is \"speakable\" and droppable); this item must keep text-readers of every changed file regardless of blocks.\n\nKnown gaps carried over (block_hash.py docstring): a `# pragma: no cover` edit changes no digest (must force the file-level rule for that file); a shadowing second `def f` selects nothing; module-level import-time code is credited to every test in the file, so a module-level edit still keeps the whole file's touchers.\n\nDone when: a one-line body edit to an io_boundary.py function, on a fresh index, runs only that function's observed tests plus the file's text-readers, the coverage verdict is green, the manifest is byte-identical to the un-narrowed selection, and --narrow is on by default locally."  # e048
    fields: {}  # e048
